{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not wrong</b> — this is the prohibition of financial wrongdoing. <b>In the number of years after the Jubilee</b> — the plain sense of the verse, on its face, comes to warn about wrongdoing: when you sell or buy land, know how many years remain until the Jubilee, and according to the years the seller shall sell and the buyer shall buy, for in the end it returns to him in the Jubilee year. And if there are few years and this one sells it for a high price, then the buyer has been wronged; and if there are many years and he will consume many harvests from it, he must buy it according to the time. And this is what is stated (here): <b>\"according to the number of years of harvests he shall sell to you\"</b> — according to the count of years of harvests that will stand in the hand of the buyer, you shall sell to him.\n\nAnd our Sages expounded (Arakhin 29a): from here, that one who sells his field may not redeem it in less than two years, even if there are three harvests in those two years. And this does not depart from the plain sense — \"the number of years of harvests\" and not of blight, and \"years\" is minimally two. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:15). And in truth this is correct in the settled reading of the verse.\n\nBut our Sages said (Bava Metzia 56a) that there is no law of wrongdoing (ona'ah) for land, as it is stated: <b>\"or acquire from the hand of your fellow\"</b> — a thing acquired from hand to hand. And this verse, according to its plain sense and according to its midrash, according to the Rav, deals with land. But perforce we need to bend verses from their plain sense and say that each verse stands on its own. It says: <b>\"And when you sell a sale to your fellow, or acquire from the hand of your fellow\"</b> — a thing acquired from hand to hand — <b>\"you shall not wrong one another\"</b>. And it returns and says: by the number of years after the Jubilee you acquire from him the harvests, and according to the number of years of harvests he sells them to you; according to the years you increase and decrease, for in any case you return it to him in the Jubilee. And all of this is a warning regarding the Jubilee, that they must always be careful in it. Then it returned and said: <b>\"And you shall not wrong one another\"</b> (Leviticus 25:17) — in words.\n\nAnd I think yet another reasoning: that surely one who wrongs his fellow intentionally transgresses a negative prohibition, whether in movable property or in land — for concerning these the verse spoke: <b>\"you shall not wrong one another in the number of years after the Jubilee\"</b>, which warns that they should buy and sell according to the years and not wrong one another. But our Sages introduced, regarding the wrongdoing of overcharging, the rule of the sixth part (sheitut) of the purchase price and the nullification of the purchase when it exceeds a sixth. And from this alone they excluded land, because overcharging in land, even when it exceeds a sixth, is forgiven — just as it is forgiven for movable property when it is less than a sixth, even though it is forbidden to wrong intentionally. But it is not the way of people to nullify their sale on account of such a small overcharge. And the Sages expounded: because the verse states <b>\"And when you sell a sale to your fellow, or acquire\"</b> — a thing acquired from hand to hand — <b>\"you shall not wrong one another\"</b>, we learn that wrongdoing has a specific rule in movable property that does not apply to land, namely the return of the money. But the warning of the negative prohibition applies to all of them. Therefore it says <b>\"And when you sell a sale\"</b> in plural language — to one who sells land and to one who sells movable property; <b>\"or acquire from the hand of your fellow\"</b> — the singular among them, the one selling movable property from hand to hand. And to all of them it says <b>\"you shall not wrong\"</b>; and since it singled out and separated movable property, it elaborated upon it the law of wrongdoing, namely the return of the payment. And this is a sound matter according to the midrashim that our Sages received from the Torah's hints.\n\nAnd perhaps all of this is an asmakhta (a scriptural support for a rabbinic law), for the negative prohibition warns both in land and in movable property, while the return of the money is by oral tradition (kabbalah) in movable property and not in land. As they said (Bava Kamma 14b): \"a matter equivalent to all — money\" — for the measurements are all in terms of a sixth part and more than a sixth part according to the assessments of people; and why should land not be excluded from this rule? And they did exclude from it the utensils of a householder, and said (Bava Metzia 51a): they taught only regarding one who buys from a merchant, but regarding one who buys from a householder there is no wrongdoing, because the way of householders is that they do not sell their utensils.\n\nAnd it is also possible to say that the verse warns that they should know the number of years until the Jubilee, and according to them they shall sell and buy, and not wrong one another — to mislead him in the count, or to mislead him in the sale so that he calculates that it is a permanent acquisition (le-chalutin) and thereby deceives him. Rather, both of them should know, and each should inform the other of the count, for the sale is by the number of years until the Jubilee. For even in land there is wrongdoing when one deceives in the measure or the count, even in less than a sixth, and all the more so in movable property."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:12.039454565Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}